        {"id":"harvard_vi4_211","series":"Band VI 4 (1677 - 1690)","seq":211,"text":"[[816.1]] <b>173.  Specimen calculi coincidentium [April bis Oktober 1686 (?)]</b>\n [[816.2]] <tab/>[April bis Oktober 1686 (?)] </p>\n<p> [[816.21]] 1) <b>Eadem</b> vel <b>Coincidentia</b> sunt quae sibi ubique substitui possunt salva\n[[816.22]] veritate. <b>Diversa</b> quae non possunt. <Fn><i>Am Rande</i>: Hinc etiam demonstrari potest, si duo coincidentia reperiantur in aliqua propositione, posse loca eorum permutari, quanquam et possit unum eorum per alterum vel omnino, vel quantum lubet, tolli.</Fn> </p>\n<p> [[816.23]] 2) <i>A</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>B</i> significat <i>A</i> et <i>B</i> esse eadem. </p>\n<p> [[816.24]] 3) <i>A non</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>B</i> significat <i>A</i> et <i>B</i> esse diversa. </p>\n<p> [[816.25]] 4) Si <i>A non</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>B</i>, etiam <i>B non</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>A</i>. </p>\n<p> [[816.26]] 5) Si <i>A</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>B</i> et <i>B</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>C</i>, etiam <i>A</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>C</i>, per 1, facta substitutione. </p>\n<p> [[817.1]] 6) Si <i>A</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>B</i> et <i>B non</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>C</i>, etiam <i>A non</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>C</i>, per 5. Hinc per 6 et 4 si <i>A</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>B</i> et <i>B non</i>\n[[817.2]] ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>C</i>, erit <i>C non</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> [<i>A</i>]. </p>\n<p> [[817.3]] 7, 8) <i>A</i> significat determinatum, <i>Y</i> vel <i>Z</i>, vel alia litera posterior significat indeterminatum,\n[[817.4]] etiam Nihil, si conditiones appositae non obstent. </p>\n<p> [[817.5]] 9, 10) <i>A</i> + <i>Y</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>C</i> significat <i>A</i> <b>inesse</b> <i>C</i>, seu <i>C</i> <b>continere</b> <i>A</i>. </p>\n<p> [[817.6]] 11) <i>A</i> + <i>A</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>A</i> significat <i>A</i> esse aliquod determinatum seu unicum, seu idem sibi ipsi\n[[817.7]] additum nihil novum facit. Hinc ut obiter dicam, quia aequalium eadem magnitudo est,\n[[817.8]] ideo si aequalia sibi addantur non dicendum est eorum magnitudines addi, sed ipsas res,\n[[817.9]] fit enim nova magnitudo. Hinc sequitur nec magnitudinem esse Numerum, nec magnitudinem\n[[817.10]] aut rationem unam alterius esse partem, nec posse sibi addi. Nec numerus cum\n[[817.11]] aequali Numero idem est, solent tamen saepe pro ipsis rebus vel saltem numeris ratio aut\n[[817.12]] magnitudo sumi. At <i>Y</i> + <i>Y non</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>Y</i> significat <i>Y</i> plura esse <i>Y</i>. </p>\n<p> [[817.13]] 12, 13) Hinc si <i>A</i> significet determinatum et <i>Y</i> indeterminatum, axiomata sunt <i>A</i> + <i>A</i>\n[[817.14]] ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>A</i> et <i>Y</i> + <i>Y non</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>Y</i>. </p>\n<p> [[817.15]] 14) Duo <i>Y</i> diversa ita soleo exprimere <i>Y</i> et (<i>Y</i>). Si vero tractemus <i>Y</i> et adhuc <i>Y</i>, seu <i>Y</i>\n[[817.16]] et (<i>Y</i>), reperiamusque <i>Y</i> + (<i>Y</i>) ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>Y</i>, erit <i>Y</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> (<i>Y</i>). Si vero reperiamus <i>Y</i> + (<i>Y</i>) <i>non</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>Y</i>, erit\n[[817.17]] <i>Y non</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> (<i>Y</i>). </p>\n<p> [[817.18]] 15) Et generaliter si <i>A</i> + <i>B</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>A</i> et <i>B</i> sit aliquid erit <i>B</i> in <i>A</i>. </p>\n<p> [[817.19]] 16) Item si <i>A</i> + <i>B</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>A</i> et <i>B</i> non in <i>A</i>, <i>B</i> erit Nihil. </p>\n<p> [[817.20]] 17) Non Nihil est aliquid, et non aliquid est Nihil. </p>\n<p> [[817.21]] 18) Si <i>A non</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>A</i> erit <i>A</i> <b>impossibile</b>. Unde et si <i>A</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>B</i> et <i>A</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>non B</i>, tunc <i>A</i> erit\n[[817.22]] <b>impossibile</b> per 18 et 6. </p>\n<p> [[817.23]] 19) Hinc quod neque Nihil est, neque aliquid, id impossibile est per 17 et 18. </p>\n<p> [[817.24]] Notandum: omnem Terminum ut <i>A</i> vel <i>B</i>, intelligi aliquid, et possibile, nisi contrarium\n[[817.25]] admoneatur aut probetur. </p>\n<p> [[817.26]] 20) Si <i>A</i> est Nihil et <i>B</i> est Nihil, erit <i>A</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>B</i>, seu duo Nihila coincidunt. </p>\n<p> [[817.27]] 21) Si <i>A</i> est Nihil et <i>B</i> est Nihil, erit <i>A</i> + <i>B</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>A</i> per [20] et 11, seu nihil additum\n[[817.28]] nihilo facit Nihil. </p>\n<p> [[818.1]] 22) Si <i>A</i> + <i>A</i> non ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>A</i> erit <i>A</i> impossibile, seu impossibile est quod additum sibi ipsi\n[[818.2]] facit novum. Nam pono <i>A</i> esse determinatum seu unum certum. Unde per 11, <i>A</i> + <i>A</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>A</i>.\n[[818.3]] Jam <i>A</i> + <i>A non</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>A</i> ex hyp. Ergo per 6, <i>A non</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>A</i>. Notandum <i>A</i> ne quidem hoc casu fore\n[[818.4]] Nihil, nam et si nihilo apponatur nihilum coincidunt, quia per 20, Nihilum Nihilo\n[[818.5]] coincidit. </p>\n<p> [[818.6]] 23) Si <i>A</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>B</i> etiam <i>A</i> + <i>C</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>B</i> + <i>C</i>. Nam si in <i>A</i> + <i>C</i> pro <i>A</i> substituas <i>B</i>, ex defin.\n[[818.7]] Eorundem, fit <i>B</i> + <i>C</i>. </p>\n<p> [[818.8]] 24) Continens [continentis] est continens [contenti], seu quod inest inexistenti, inest\n[[818.9]] ei cui inexistit; seu contentum contenti est contentum continentis, seu si <i>A</i> est in <i>B</i>, et <i>B</i>\n[[818.10]] est in <i>C</i>, etiam <i>A</i> est in <i>C</i>. Nam <i>A</i> + <i>Y</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>B</i> ex hyp. per 9, et similiter <i>B</i> + <i>Z</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>C</i>. Ergo (per\n[[818.11]] substit.) <i>A</i> + <i>Y</i> + <i>Z</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>C</i>. Sit <i>Y</i> + <i>Z</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>V</i> (per 25). Erit <i>A</i> + <i>V</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>C</i>. Ergo <i>A</i> est in <i>C</i> per 9.\n[[818.12]] Quod Erat Dem. </p>\n<p> [[818.13]] 25) Postulati instar est, ut liceat pro pluribus quotcunque, ponere unum aliquod ipsis\n[[818.14]] collectis coincidens. Hoc tamen ostendi potest, ex alio postulato clariore, quod pro\n[[818.15]] pluribus ut <i>A</i> et <i>B</i> possit poni unum <i>C</i>, ita ut sit <i>A</i> + <i>B</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>C</i>. Si scilicet nihil in uno\n[[818.16]] reperiatur, quod sit in alio, verbi gratia si post ea omnia sumta quae sunt in <i>A</i> sumamus ea\n[[818.17]] omnia quae sunt in <i>B</i>, et ita omnia simul collecta dicamus constituere <i>C</i>, eorum aggregatum\n[[818.18]] in quo unumquodque eorum insit. Sed hinc tamen sequitur idem fieri posse etiamsi\n[[818.19]] <i>B</i> et <i>A</i> habeant commune aliquid quod insit utrique <i>A</i> et <i>B</i>. Ponamus enim id esse <i>D</i>, et <i>A</i>\n[[818.20]] esse ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>D</i> + <i>E</i>, et <i>B</i> esse ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>D</i> + <i>F</i>, ita ut <i>D, E, F</i> nullum habeant commune contentum,\n[[818.21]] dico fieri posse <i>A</i> + <i>B</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>C</i>. Nam fiet <i>D</i> + <i>E</i> + <i>D</i> + <i>F</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>C</i>. Jam <i>D</i> + <i>D</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>D</i>. Ergo fiet\n[[818.22]] <i>D</i> + <i>E</i> + <i>F</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>C</i>, quod fieri posse diximus, quia <i>D</i> et <i>E</i> et <i>F</i> nil habent commune\n[[818.23]] contentum. </p>\n<p> [[818.24]] 26) Hic obiter notari potest discrimen inter viam et lineam; si punctum mobile tendat\n[[818.25]] per aliquam lineam a puncto <i>A</i> ad punctum <i>B</i>, et per eandem redeat a puncto <i>B</i> ad\n[[818.26]] punctum <i>A</i>, linea quidem percursa non erit major, quam si non rediisset, nihil enim\n[[818.27]] novum est in regressu quod non fuerit in itione, et idem sibi ipsi additum non facit\n[[818.28]] novum, per 11, at via percursa erit duplo longior; nisi quis malit viam pro ipsa linea\n[[818.29]] sumere. </p>\n<p> [[819.1]] 27) Quoties literam aliquam novam assumimus, tunc possumus quodvis, quod non\n[[819.2]] est impossibile de ipsa asserere. Sed cum ea litera jam ante adhibita fuit in eodem calculo\n[[819.3]] vel ratiocinio, non licet, nisi ostendamus ea quae nunc de ea asserimus, cum prioribus\n[[819.4]] esse compatibilia, quo ostenso id asserere de ea licet. Haec observatio etiam instar\n[[819.5]] postulati esse potest, et pendet ex natura nostrae characteristicae. V.g. si habuerimus\n[[819.6]] <i>D</i> + <i>C</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>A</i> et <i>D non</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>C</i>, <Fn><i>Am Rande gestr.</i>: incommunicantia</Fn> et tam <i>D</i> quam [<i>C</i>] sint aliquid et possibile; non licet postea\n[[819.7]] ponere <i>D</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>A</i>, sed nihil prohibet ponere <i>E</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>A</i>. Item si sit <i>D</i> + <i>C</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>A</i> et <i>F</i> + <i>G</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>H</i>, nil\n[[819.8]] prohibet novam facere positionem in iisdem literis modo priori compatibilem; ut <i>F</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character>\n[[819.9]] <i>G</i> + <i>C</i>. At si scripsissemus <i>F</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>H</i> + <i>C</i>, id foret priori incompatibile. Sin novam assumsissemus\n[[819.10]] literam in nova assertione, nihil esset timendum. </p>\n<p> [[819.11]] 28) Nihilum sive ponatur sive non, nihil refert. seu <i>A</i> + <i>Nih.</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>A</i>. </p>\n<p> [[819.12]] 29) <Fn><i>Am Rande gestr.</i>: incommunicantia</Fn> Signo + hactenus sumus usi ad designandum unum collectivum fieri ex pluribus;\n[[819.13]] in quo plura insint, et quod ipsis simul sumtis coincidat. Nunc signo ─ utemur ad\n[[819.14]] designandum, aliqua ab alio esse detrahenda, ut contrarium fiat signi +. Itaque si <i>A</i> + <i>B</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character>\n[[819.15]] <i>C</i>, erit <i>A</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>C</i> ─ <i>B</i>, et <i>A</i> dicitur <b>Residuum</b>. Sed opus est <i>A</i> et <i>B</i> nihil habere commune.\n[[819.16]] Nam exempli causa <i>A</i> + <i>A</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>A</i>, ergo fieret <i>A</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>A</i> ─ <i>A</i>. Jam (per 30) <i>A</i> ─ <i>A</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>Nihilo</i>, ergo\n[[819.17]] fieret <i>A</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>Nihilo</i> contra Hyp. </p>\n<p> [[819.18]] 30) <i>C</i> ─ <i>C</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>Nihilo</i>. Nam <i>C</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>C</i> + <i>Nih.</i> per 28. Ergo <i>C</i> ─ <i>C</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>Nih.</i> per [29]. </p>\n<p> [[819.19]] 31) Si ab aliquo [<i>B</i>] detrahi jubeatur [<i>C</i>] quod ipsi non inest, tunc residuum <i>A</i> seu\n[[819.20]] <i>B</i> ─ <i>C</i> erit res semiprivativa, et si apponatur alicui <i>D</i>, tunc <i>D</i> + <i>A</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>E</i> significat <i>D</i> quidem\n[[819.21]] et <i>B</i> esse ponenda, sed tamen a <i>D</i> prius esse removendum <i>C</i>, si quidem ei inest. Quod si\n[[819.22]] insit, seu si <i>D</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>C</i> + <i>F</i> fiet <i>E</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>C</i> + <i>F</i> + <i>B</i> ─ <i>C</i>. Hoc est (per 30) <i>E</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>F</i> + <i>B</i>. Unde <i>E</i> fit res\n[[819.23]] positiva, posito <i>F</i> et <i>B</i> esse positivas. Sed si <i>C</i> non insit ipsi <i>D</i>, manet etiam <i>E</i> res\n[[819.24]] semiprivativa. </p>\n<p> [[820.1]] 32) Omnis positio quae inest in <i>E</i> sit <i>G</i>, et omnis privatio quae inest in <i>E</i> sit <i>C</i>, sic ut\n[[820.2]] sit <i>E</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>G</i> ─ <i>C</i>. Jam omne quod commune est ipsi <i>G</i> et <i>C</i> sit <i>H</i>, et sit <i>G</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>H</i> + <i>L</i>, et <i>C</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character>\n[[820.3]] <i>H</i> + <i>M</i>, fiet <i>E</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>H</i> + <i>L</i> ─ <i>H</i> ─ <i>M</i> seu (per 30) <i>E</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>L</i> ─ <i>M</i> et <i>L</i> atque <i>M</i> nihil amplius\n[[820.4]] habebunt commune, quod si jam <i>L</i> et <i>M</i> (incommunicantia) ambo sint aliquid positivum,\n[[820.5]] erit <i>E</i> <b>res semiprivativa</b>; sin sit <i>M</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>Nih.</i> erit <i>E</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>L</i> seu <i>E</i> erit <b>res positiva</b>, si\n[[820.6]] scilicet id omne <i>H</i> quod inest toti privationi <i>C</i>, insit etiam positioni <i>G</i>; denique sin sit <i>L</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character>\n[[820.7]] <i>Nih.</i> erit <i>E</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> ─ <i>M</i> seu <i>E</i> erit <b>res privativa</b>, si nempe omne <i>H</i> quod est in tota positione\n[[820.8]] <i>G</i> insit etiam privationi <i>C</i>. </p>\n<p> [[820.9]] 33) Hic cuicunque apponi potest privatio cujuscunque, est instar postulati, sit <i>A</i>, et\n[[820.10]] <i>B</i>, scribi potest <i>A</i> ─ <i>B</i>. </p>\n<p> [[820.11]] 34) Si <i>A</i> + <i>B</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>D</i> + <i>C</i> et <i>A</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>D</i> erit <i>B</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>C</i>. Seu quibus apponendo coincidentia fiunt\n[[820.12]] coincidentia, ea ipsa sunt coincidentia. <Fn><i>Am Rande</i>: Imo non sequitur nisi in incommunicantibus.</Fn> Nam scribatur <i>A</i> + <i>B</i> ─ <i>A</i> (per 33) erit <i>B</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character>\n[[820.13]] <i>A</i> + <i>B</i> ─ <i>D</i> (per 30). Ergo (pro <i>A</i> + <i>B</i> substituendo coincidens <i>D</i> + <i>C</i>) fiet <i>B</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> [<i>D</i>] + <i>C</i> ─ <i>D</i>\n[[820.14]] id est <i>C</i> (per 30). Ergo <i>B</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>C</i>. Quod erat dem. </p>\n<p> [[820.15]] 35) Si a coincidentibus auferas coincidentia fiunt coincidentia. Si [<i>A</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>D</i> et] <i>B</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>C</i>\n[[820.16]] erit <i>A</i> ─ <i>B</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>D</i> ─ <i>C</i>. Nam si ad <i>A</i> ─ <i>B</i> et <i>D</i> ─ <i>C</i> addas coincidentia <i>B</i> et <i>C</i>, fiunt <i>A</i> et <i>D</i>\n[[820.17]] coincidentia. Ergo <i>A</i> ─ <i>B</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>D</i> ─ <i>C</i>, per 34. Seu ^#6+<i>A</i> ─ <i>B</i>^#6- + <i>B</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> ^#6+<i>D</i> ─ <i>C</i>^#6- + <i>C</i> (^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>D</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>A</i>) et <i>B</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character>\n[[820.18]] <i>C</i>. Ergo per 34, <i>A</i> ─ <i>B</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>D</i> ─ <i>C</i>. </p>\n<p> [[821.1]] 36) Insunt in aliquo non tantum partes sed et alia, ut circulo inest non tantum\n[[821.2]] quadratum inscriptum, sed et latus quadrati inscripti. Quadratum quidem est pars ejus,\n[[821.3]] sed latus quadrati non est pars ejus. Sed pars ab alio inexistente non potest discerni, nisi\n[[821.4]] accedente consideratione similis vel congrui, de qua suo loco. </p>\n<p> [[821.5]] 37) Speciatim consideranda sunt <b>contenta ejusdem incommunicantia inter\n[[821.6]] se</i>. Si sit <i>L</i> in <i>A</i>, et <i>M</i> in <i>B</i>, atque inde sequitur <i>L non</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>M</i>, dicentur <i>A</i> et [<i>B</i>]\n[[821.7]] <b>incommunicantia.</b> </p>\n<p> [[821.8]] 38) Cum aliquid dicitur coincidere pluribus, saepius incommunicantia intelligere\n[[821.9]] soleo. Seu contenta ejusdem quae continens constituunt, intelligi solent <b>incommunicantia</b>. </p>\n<p> [[821.11]] 39) Si <i>A</i> et <i>B</i> incommunicantia et <i>A</i> + <i>B</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>C</i>, non erit <i>A</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>C</i>. Nam alioqui erit <i>A</i> + <i>B</i>\n[[821.12]] ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>A</i>. Ergo (per 15) erit <i>B</i> in <i>A</i>, contra Hypoth. aut <i>B</i> erit Nihil, quod etiam est contra\n[[821.13]] Hypothesin. Communi sermone, si contenta incommunicantia simul coincidant continenti,\n[[821.14]] non potest unum eorum coincidere continenti. <Fn><i>Darüber am Rande</i>: Si <i>A</i> et <i>B</i> nihil habent commune, itemque <i>L</i> et <i>M</i>, et <i>A</i> non sit ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>L</i>, nec <i>B</i> erit <i>M</i>. Si <i>L</i> et <i>M</i> habent aliquid commune et <i>A</i> + <i>B</i> sit ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>L</i> + <i>M</i>, poterit esse <i>A</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>L</i>, licet <i>B</i> non sit ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>M</i>, ut si <i>A</i> + <i>B</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>A</i> + <i>B</i> + <i>A</i>, quod fiet si <i>L</i> sit <i>A</i> et <i>M</i> sit <i>A</i> + <i>B</i>. Si <i>A</i> et <i>B</i> incommunicantia item <i>L</i> et <i>M</i>, et nullum horum coincidat ulli illorum, non potest simul utrumque utrique inesse, sed si <i>A</i> inest <i>L</i>, non erit <i>B</i> in <i>M</i>. </Fn> </p>\n<p> [[821.15]] 40) Si <i>A</i> + <i>B</i> + <i>C</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>L</i> singula contenta, ut <i>A</i> vel <i>B</i> vel <i>C</i>, voco contenta <b>constituentia</b>,\n[[821.16]] ipsum autem <i>L</i> <b>constitutum</b>. </p>\n<p> [[822.1]] 41) <Fn><i>Darunter am Rande</i>: Coincidentia assignare talia Efficere ut ab ipsis detrahendo eadem, residua non coincidant.  <tab/><i>G</i> + <i>M</i> + <tab/><i>M</i> + <i>H</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <tab/><i>G</i> + <i>M</i> + <i>H</i>  <tab/><picture>9011</picture> <tab/><picture>9011</picture> <tab/><picture>9011</picture>  <tab/> <i>A</i> <tab/> <i>B</i> <tab/> <i>A</i> </Fn> Si <i>M</i> est in <i>C</i> et <i>N</i> est in <i>C</i> erit <i>M</i> + <i>N</i> in <i>C</i>, seu cui singula insunt etiam ex ipsis\n[[822.2]] constitutum inest. Nam quia <i>M</i> est in <i>C</i>, ergo <i>M</i> + <i>R</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>C</i>. Similiter <i>N</i> + <i>S</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>C</i>. Ergo\n[[822.3]] <i>M</i> + <i>R</i> + <i>N</i> + <i>S</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>C</i> + <i>C</i>. Ergo (per 11) <i>M</i> + <i>R</i> + <i>N</i> + <i>S</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>C</i>. Ergo <i>M</i> + <i>N</i> in <i>C</i>. Quod erat\n[[822.4]] dem. </p>\n<p> [[822.5]] 42) Si <i>M</i> est in <i>A</i>, et <i>N</i> est in <i>B</i> erit <i>M</i> + <i>N</i> in <i>A</i> + <i>B</i>. Seu constitutum ex contentis\n[[822.6]] inest constituto ex continentibus. Hoc ita demonstro: <i>M</i> est in <i>A</i> (ex hyp.). Ergo in <i>A</i> + <i>B</i>\n[[822.7]] (per 24). Similiter <i>N</i> est in <i>B</i> (ex hyp.). Ergo <i>N</i> est in <i>A</i> + <i>B</i> (per 24). Jam si <i>M</i> est in <i>A</i> + <i>B</i>\n[[822.8]] et <i>N</i> est in <i>A</i> + <i>B</i>, erit (per 41) <i>M</i> + <i>N</i> in <i>A</i> + <i>B</i>. Quod erat dem. <Fn><i>Am Rande</i>: Si <i>A</i> sit in <i>B</i>, et <i>B</i> sit in <i>A</i>, tunc <i>A</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>B</i>. Nam <i>A</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>B</i> + <i>L</i> et <i>B</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>A</i> + <i>M</i>. Ergo <i>A</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>B</i> + <i>A</i> + <i>M</i>. </Fn> </p>\n<p> [[822.9]] 43) Si <i>L</i> est in <i>A</i> + <i>B</i> et <i>L</i> non est in <i>A</i>, nec in <i>B</i>, poterit assumi <i>L</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>M</i> + <i>N</i>, sic ut sit\n[[822.10]] <i>M</i> in <i>A</i> et <i>N</i> in <i>B</i>. Vel familiari sermone, si quid sit in constituto, nec sit in uno\n[[822.11]] constituentium, erit partim in uno partim in alio. Hoc ita probo, quia alioqui etiam si quis\n[[822.12]] cognosceret omnia quae sunt in <i>L</i>, non posset ostendere <i>L</i> esse in <i>A</i> + <i>B</i>, cum tamen\n[[822.13]] omnis veritas ex cognitis rebus ostendi possit. Sed quia haec ratiocinatio abest a rigore\n[[822.14]] demonstrationis, possemus hanc propositionem assumere instar axiomatis, sed praestat\n[[822.15]] tamen quaerere demonstrationem, quia huc usque omnia demonstravimus. Sed ad hanc\n[[822.16]] rem novis opus est considerationibus quas nunc exponemus. </p>\n<p> [[822.17]] 44) <b>Inexistens ultimum</b> voco quod ita inest, ut nihil ipsi amplius insit, seu si <i>L</i>\n[[822.18]] sit inexistens ultimum, et assumatur <i>A</i> + <i>B</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>L</i>, erit <i>A</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>B</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>L</i>. Tale est punctum in\n[[822.19]] spatio, instans in tempore. </p>\n","number":"173.","title":"Specimen calculi coincidentium","date":"[April bis Oktober 1686 (?)]"}
      </script>

      <!-- Detailed scholarly view with line numbers -->
